  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 323 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nBehind a sweeping cold front skies are becoming mostly clear \nwith dry air advecting in, while temperatures have returned to \nseasonal values with lows in the 20s and 30s tonight. The \noverall pattern through the next week results in persistent \nprecipitation chances as the region rides in a baroclinic zone \nwith southwest flow aloft. This also favors no significant \nfluctuation from these near average temperatures, though \ntrending slightly warmer for the second half of the week.\n\nEnsemble guidance shows increased potential for a hybrid-type, \nprogressive shortwave and associated low pressure system to \nmove through the Northern Plains and Upper Midwest Monday into\nTuesday. Confidence is high for periods of rain for areas south\nof US-2, while more moderate confidence exists for the extent \nof moisture into the northern zones as well as any accumulation\nof snow occurring overnight. This will largely be depending on \nforcing and having sufficient rates to accumulating more than a \ndusting on elevated or grassy surfaces. The most favorable area\nfor seeing snow is west of the Red River Valley Monday night, \nthough most model solutions support areas with heavier \nprecipitation rates remaining as rain or a mix.",
